# NOTICE — Protocol & Clean-Room Attribution

## Reticulum Wire Protocol Attribution & Scope
BIFROST — The Reticulum Ghost Browser is an **experimental application-layer HTTP/DPP browser (`v0.1.0-preview`)** built on top of the MIT Rust `Reticulum-rs` library and `citadel-rns-rs`. It is **not** a full Reticulum protocol implementation.

The **Reticulum Protocol** was invented and dedicated to the Public Domain in 2016 by Danish engineer **Mark Qvist** (<https://reticulum.network>), whose Python implementation (<https://github.com/markqvist/Reticulum>) remains the sole authoritative Reference Implementation. We give full credit to Mark Qvist for creating Reticulum.

## Clean-Room Rust Application Stack & Intended Use
BIFROST is designed strictly for **EU ESPR / CIRPASS-2 Digital Product Passports, industrial resilience, and defensive sovereign privacy** (non-weaponized, zero harm to humans). It does **not** copy, derive from, or train AI models on Mark Qvist's Python `RNS` source code or documentation.

Full wire-level interoperability testing against the official Python `RNS` reference node—and evaluating community-recognized **Quad4 `Reticulum-Go`** as an optional transport backend—is part of our active engineering roadmap.

All Rust crates used by BIFROST are permissive open-source:
- `citadel-rns-rs` (<https://github.com/whattio/citadel-rns-rs>, MIT License)
- `reticulum-rs` (MIT License)
- `ed25519-dalek` & `sha2` (RustCrypto, MIT / Apache-2.0)
- `wry` & `tao` (Tauri Programme within The Commons Conservancy, MIT / Apache-2.0)

